WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.437
Sparrow: Whew! It is so hot here!

2
00:00:07.438 --> 00:00:10.812
Sparrow: I have been flying over this desert all day.

3
00:00:10.813 --> 00:00:13.812
Sparrow: I need to find a cool place to rest.

4
00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:29.022
Sparrow: Look, there's a hole in the ground. I'll rest there.

5
00:00:29.023 --> 00:00:31.981
Mouse: Who are you?

6
00:00:31.982 --> 00:00:35.614
Sparrow: Oh, I'm sorry. I am a sparrow.

7
00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:41.984
Sparrow: This desert is so dry and hot that I did not expect [n3@to see any animals living here!

8
00:00:41.985 --> 00:00:48.099
Mouse: I am a desert mouse, and there are a lot of animals here.

9
00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.442
Sparrow: Really?

10
00:00:49.443 --> 00:00:54.819
Mouse: Yes. The days are very hot and dry, but the nights are cold.

11
00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:59.037
Mouse: So most desert animals do not move around during the day.

12
00:00:59.038 --> 00:01:02.368
Sparrow: But how do they endure so much heat?

13
00:01:02.369 --> 00:01:06.923
Sparrow: It is so hot that I am about to die!

14
00:01:06.924 --> 00:01:12.262
Mouse: Well, some desert animals use parts of their bodies to cool off.

15
00:01:12.263 --> 00:01:20.578
Mouse: For example, desert owls open their mouths [n3@wide and flutter their throats.

16
00:01:20.579 --> 00:01:24.674
Mouse: The jackrabbit's ears are long and have many blood vessels.

17
00:01:24.675 --> 00:01:28.878
Mouse: The heat is released through the blood vessels in its long ears.

18
00:01:28.879 --> 00:01:32.829
Sparrow: Oh, that's interesting!

19
00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:38.027
Sparrow: Up north, I have seen other rabbits, and [n3@their ears are much shorter.

20
00:01:38.028 --> 00:01:43.358
Mouse: Yes, up north, they do not need long ears to survive.

21
00:01:43.359 --> 00:01:48.112
Mouse: All over the world, animals are suited to their different habitats.

22
00:01:48.113 --> 00:01:51.910
Mouse: For example, most desert animals are light-colored.

23
00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:55.806
Mouse: Their lighter colors keep them from getting really hot too.

24
00:01:55.807 --> 00:01:58.837
Sparrow: Then what about the darker animals?

25
00:01:58.838 --> 00:02:03.580
Sparrow: They would get much hotter than light-colored animals. Right?

26
00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:09.311
Mouse: You are right. Vultures are dark-colored, but they [n3@wet their legs with their urine.

27
00:02:09.312 --> 00:02:11.983
Mouse: The urine cools the blood in their legs.

28
00:02:11.984 --> 00:02:14.835
Mouse: The cooler blood goes back through their bodies.

29
00:02:14.836 --> 00:02:20.926
Sparrow: I see. They are really amazing!

30
00:02:20.927 --> 00:02:24.511
Sparrow: Then how do desert animals find water?

31
00:02:24.512 --> 00:02:26.991
Sparrow: You do need water, don't you?

32
00:02:26.992 --> 00:02:32.909
Mouse: Of course! We desert creatures get water from plants.

33
00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.402
Mouse: The juicy cactus is a favorite! Have a piece!

34
00:02:36.403 --> 00:02:40.011
Sparrow: But won't I get stuck by the thorns?

35
00:02:40.012 --> 00:02:45.920
Mouse: Not really. Go ahead, just be careful.

36
00:02:45.921 --> 00:02:53.565
Sparrow: Hmm, not bad! By the way, how do you stay cool in this desert?

37
00:02:53.566 --> 00:02:56.500
Mouse: I live in an underground den like this.

38
00:02:56.501 --> 00:02:59.182
Mouse: I close the opening when it is very hot.

39
00:02:59.183 --> 00:03:00.853
That keeps out the heat.

40
00:03:00.854 --> 00:03:03.773
Mouse: Then I can save the moisture from my breath.

41
00:03:03.774 --> 00:03:09.390
Sparrow: Wonderful! Desert animals are really amazing.

42
00:03:09.391 --> 00:03:11.585
Sparrow: But I might not survive here.

43
00:03:11.586 --> 00:03:15.400
Mouse: That's true. The desert gets very little rain.

44
00:03:15.401 --> 00:03:19.127
Mouse: It gets less than 25 centimeters of rain a year!

45
00:03:19.128 --> 00:03:23.627
Mouse: So all the animals in the desert have special [n3@ways to help them survive.

46
00:03:23.628 --> 00:03:25.249
Mouse: But you have wings.

47
00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:29.923
Mouse: You can fly to a place where you will survive well.

48
00:03:29.924 --> 00:03:31.688
Sparrow: Yes, you are right!

49
00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:33.425
I have to leave now.

50
00:03:33.426 --> 00:03:37.492
Sparrow: Thank you for telling me about desert animals. Good-bye!

51
00:03:37.493 --> 00:03:41.064
Mouse: My pleasure! Good-bye!
